## 2. Source architecture

Use one Gradle `app` module and bounded packages:

```text
com.internal.tracker
├── core
│   ├── audit
│   ├── database
│   ├── device
│   ├── security
│   └── time
├── tracking
├── trip
├── identity
├── vehicle
├── expense
├── mail
├── maintenance
└── ui
```

Dependency direction is UI -> domain/use case -> repository -> infrastructure. Domain logic must not import Compose, Android UI, DAO implementation, or JavaMail. ViewModels never call DAO/SMTP directly.

Required injectable abstractions include `BusinessClock`, `UuidSource`, `LocationSource`, `DeviceIdentitySource`, `FileStager`, `MailTransport`, `WorkScheduler`, and `StorageProbe`.

Use durable intent/state for external side effects. A Room transaction can atomically create a Trip and mail intent, but cannot atomically send SMTP or write an external file. Workers claim eligible durable work and record every transition.

## 3. Encrypted database

### 3.1 Opening and keys

On first launch:

1. generate a random SQLCipher passphrase;
2. wrap it with an Android Keystore AES key;
3. persist only wrapped material;
4. open Room with SQLCipher;
5. enable WAL and foreign keys.

Never invoke destructive fallback. A failed unwrap/open transitions to a blocking Recovery state while preserving files.

